  "notes": "A tip-drive helicopter (PLAN Phase 5): the rotor shaft is FREE - no motor on it anywhere - and the head is driven entirely by two small propellers mounted at 92% radius on the blades, thrusting along the blades' direction of travel. Their torque enters the shaft as blade-frame force, never as shaft torque, so the airframe reaction is close to zero: NO TAIL ROTOR, and the hover test asserts the yaw barely drifts. The fin is for weathervaning in translation, not anti-torque.\n\nEXPORT NOTES — contact spring numbers are Flightline-typical, not converted — the two contact models differ. drag CdA per axis estimated from the drag parts (x, 2x, 3x). head speed om0 set to 80 rad/s (typical for tip drive), not derived. tip drive: 2 props at 0.736 m, Tmax estimated 14.72 N each. no yaw device in the source; a 0.74 N·m mast motor added so the pedal works — Flightline's own tip-drive yaws the same way.",
